{
  "dom_id": "step-q71526",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-7x$ at the point $(-1,5).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-7x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-7x)=6{x}^{2}-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(-1)=6(-1)^{2}-7=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-1)}=1.\n$$",
